162
00:08:27,056 --> 00:08:32,868
Since we're talking about coding styles and doing things the right way, let's talk about verification.

163
00:08:32,976 --> 00:08:37,536
This isn't exactly a feature or a command, it's more so a workflow and best practice.

164
00:08:37,808 --> 00:08:44,288
And I'm giving this an S tier, and if anything, a double S or even a triple S because this isn't just an AI thing.

165
00:08:44,368 --> 00:08:46,448
This is just good software engineering fundamentals.

166
00:08:46,608 --> 00:08:48,288
AI just makes this more important.

167
00:08:48,462 --> 00:08:50,208
So, what exactly is verification?

168
00:08:50,368 --> 00:08:55,088
Essentially, you're giving Cloud a way to check its own work before it considers something done.

169
00:08:55,168 --> 00:08:57,316
It actually has no idea if it's correct.

170
00:08:57,488 --> 00:09:02,448
But with verification, it can write the code and see if it's actually correct, not just telling you it's done.

171
00:09:02,608 --> 00:09:06,128
Now there's multiple ways to verify, and the most obvious one is tests.

172
00:09:06,208 --> 00:09:07,968
But make sure you do tests correctly.

173
00:09:08,174 --> 00:09:10,848
Tell Claude to write the test first and then implement.

174
00:09:11,008 --> 00:09:11,648
This is important.

175
00:09:11,822 --> 00:09:16,688
If you tell it to implement first and then write the test, it's just going to write tests that pass its own code.

176
00:09:16,814 --> 00:09:18,848
And that's not exactly testing, that's cheating.

177
00:09:19,008 --> 00:09:22,768
Make sure you don't test every single line, just test the most important things.

178
00:09:22,958 --> 00:09:29,328
I've noticed that smarter models like to test every single thing when it's really not necessary and it kind of just bloats your code base.

179
00:09:29,488 --> 00:09:32,288
Some other ways to verify are type checkers and linters.

180
00:09:32,448 --> 00:09:36,048
If your language supports it, tell Claude to run it before considering the task done.

181
00:09:36,208 --> 00:09:44,288
Two other ways you can do verification, and this is if you're doing front-end and design work, is screenshot testing and testing with the browser because Claude is literally blind.

182
00:09:44,368 --> 00:09:46,208
It can't actually see what it's making.

183
00:09:46,368 --> 00:09:52,608
With these two verification methods, Claude can actually open your app, click around, get some screenshots, and actually see what's happening.

184
00:09:52,910 --> 00:09:55,408
It's nice to see that AI models are getting good at using computers.

185
00:09:55,568 --> 00:09:57,808
You used to have to use MCPs, but now they're just built in.

186
00:09:58,048 --> 00:10:00,528
Oh right, MCP B tier.

187
00:10:00,688 --> 00:10:01,488
Actually, I'm lying.

188
00:10:01,648 --> 00:10:02,208
A tier.

189
00:10:02,368 --> 00:10:04,528
MCPs give cloud access to other tools.

190
00:10:04,654 --> 00:10:10,928
So things like GitHub, your database, Slack, checking out your analytics, your deployments, and if you do front-end work, a browser.

191
00:10:11,088 --> 00:10:20,388
Some of the ways I like to use MCPs are for fetching design inspirations, database seeding, so adding fake data to test certain features, research and documentation, and of course browser testing.

192
00:10:20,608 --> 00:10:26,628
Now, in my experience, I think you only need an MCP when Claude has to directly interact with something outside your code base.

193
00:10:26,768 --> 00:10:33,328
There is really no reason to use an MCP for things like best practices, patterns, or how to implement something because the skill can handle that.

194
00:10:33,422 --> 00:10:36,368
The best practices for MCPs are basically the same as skills.

195
00:10:36,448 --> 00:10:39,648
Don't spam them, don't install a bunch of them, only use what's necessary.

196
00:10:39,822 --> 00:10:43,088
Honestly, the big pattern with AI is only do what's necessary.

197
